@node dokgen_diagrams
@section (dokgen diagrams)

@c %start of fragment

This module turns the diagrams a documentation string carries into
images the manual can show, or into source a browser can draw.

A diagram is written in a docstring as a fenced block, the way a
markdown reader already knows.  Two languages are read: mermaid and
gnuplot.  What follows is one, and it is drawn where the manual shows
this module:

@image{diagrams/dokgen_diagrams-bc388dc4,,,Mermaid diagram}

The fence is consumed, the diagram is written next to the generated
Texinfo as @file{diagrams/NAME-HASH.mmd} (or @file{.gp} for gnuplot),
and a renderer turns it into @file{diagrams/NAME-HASH.png} or
@file{.svg}, which the API chapter references with @code{@@image}.  The
name carries a digest of the diagram's own text, so a diagram that
changed gets a file of its own and an old rendering can never be shown
as the current one.

A renderer is a command, not a build-time library: @code{mmdc} is a Node
program, and a manual should not need it to show a diagram.  Each
language has a command of its own, and a command left empty draws
nothing.  A command whose program is not installed is skipped in
silence: a diagram that cannot be drawn is still a diagram that can be
read, and a project without the renderer should not be told off on every
build.

Mermaid has a second path: when there is no image, the source is written
into the manual with a marker, and the script dokgen writes beside the
chapter draws it in the reader's browser from a bundled copy of mermaid.
That is what lets a project show its diagrams without installing
@code{mmdc} at all.  A reader without JavaScript, and every Info or
print reader, gets the source instead.  Gnuplot has no such path -- it
is a small program that draws an SVG at build time -- but its source
stays readable when the program is missing.


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

